# English captions (seconds in the ORIGINAL recording NAF007.mp3). *word* = shown in gold.
# Cleaned by hand from TurboScribe (merged SRT, transcribed only up to 23:34). 7:05-22:15 came back as Roman Urdu and was
# translated by hand: listen before publishing. Garbled stretches (4:18-6:56, 15:47-16:59, 22:45-23:00) are left out.
# "khatra / khatrat" = a stray passing thought (TurboScribe wrote "danger"); "muhmal" = useless (it wrote "patient").
CAPTIONS = [
    (0.60, 13.70, "A'udhu billahi min ash-shaytan ir-rajeem. Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Raheem. *Astaghfirullah*, Astaghfirullah al-'Azeem. La ilaha illallah."),
    (15.60, 28.80, "Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Raheem. “*Innallaha wa mala'ikatahu yusalluna 'alan-Nabi*; ya ayyuhal-ladhina amanu sallu 'alayhi wa sallimu taslima”: Allah and His angels send blessings on the Prophet ﷺ; O believers, send blessings on him and greet him with peace. Allahumma salli 'ala Sayyidina Muhammadin wa 'ala alihi wa sahbihi wa sallim."),
    (60.10, 71.30, "Sadaqallahul-Mawlal-'Azeem, wa sadaqa Rasuluhun-Nabiyyul-Kareem, wa nahnu 'ala dhalika minash-shahideen."),
    (101.60, 108.60, "Bi-hurmatil-Habib, al-Fatiha. *Page 22*."),
    (110.80, 146.40, "Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Raheem. At the end of the last lesson it says: value your *deeds*; with deeds comes *grace*. If there is no deed, there is no question of grace; if there is deed, there is grace."),
    (151.90, 175.70, "“At the time of *khatrat* (stray thoughts), this too is a way: whatever useless thought comes at the wrong time, ask for that same thought in its *positive* form.”"),
    (177.10, 192.10, "Ask for the same thought. All these unwanted thoughts keep coming; now ask *Allah* for the thoughts we *need*."),
    (193.00, 209.90, "Useless thoughts are coming: what should we do? “O Master, we are waiting for Your *grace*. Take away these useless thoughts, and give us *good thoughts*.”"),
    (211.70, 227.10, "“At the time of khatrat, whatever useless thought comes at the wrong time, ask for that same thought in its *positive* form.”"),
    (229.10, 231.70, "And pray to be saved from *bad thoughts*."),
    (233.10, 256.20, "Because it is Allah's command: *Ud'uni astajib lakum*. Ud'u means ask, ni means of Me: ask of Me. Astajib: I will *give*; lakum: to you."),
    (275.20, 283.80, "Why should deeds be valued? With deeds there is grace. When we pray, we get *reward*."),
    (323.20, 340.20, "The truth is: when we think with the *heart*, we receive inspiration; and with it our hands, feet, heart and mind all work."),
    (360.80, 370.10, "So we do two things: of the *body*, and of the *heart and soul*."),
    (416.70, 422.60, "So what do we have to do? Ask *Allah*, and ask for His blessings."),
    (425.90, 436.70, "“Whatever useless thought comes at the wrong time, ask for that thought in its *positive* form.”"),
    (437.20, 465.50, "It means: leave the useless thought that is coming, bring in good thoughts, and say: “Ya Allah, grant me *good thoughts*. It is Your command: *Ud'uni*, ask of Me; *astajib lakum*, I will answer you. You have said You will give: so stop these thoughts that are coming, and give us good ones.”"),
    (469.20, 490.10, "Allah's command is: Ud'uni astajib lakum. And: *ad-du'a mukhkh al-'ibadah*, du'a is the *core* of worship. What is the core of worship? It is du'a."),
    (490.90, 525.60, "We worship, and then we make du'a. We did the worship, but we did not *ask*; we did everything, but we did not ask. Yet Allah's command is: ask. *Ud'uni*: you ask of Me; *astajib lakum*: I will give you."),
    (526.80, 548.10, "So it is important for us to make *du'a*. Ad-du'a mukhkh al-'ibadah: the du'a we make to Allah is the *core* of our worship. The purpose of our worship is fulfilled; the worship we do is *completed* by du'a."),
    (549.50, 557.40, "There was a pact of *yaad-faramosh*."),
    (613.10, 621.30, "A pact of *yaad-faramosh* had been made: the remembrance of Allah must go on; never *forget*."),
    (621.90, 632.30, "Meaning, we had agreed on it among ourselves. We would sit together, talk, and go about our *work*."),
    (632.70, 645.20, "One of them would call out: “*Allah!*” Whoever's heart was present would say “*Alhamdulillah*”; whoever had become heedless would say “*Astaghfirullah*”."),
    (646.10, 658.30, "Whoever had become heedless: Astaghfirullah, meaning: Allah, forgive our sin. The *connection* with Allah must not break; one must not *forget* Allah."),
    (658.80, 677.90, "One of them would call out “Allah!” Whoever's heart was present would say Alhamdulillah. Whoever had *faramoshi*, meaning heedlessness: he had forgotten. Faramoshi means *forgetting*."),
    (678.60, 687.50, "Whoever had forgotten would say Astaghfirullah: *istighfar*, meaning: O my Allah, *forgive* me."),
    (688.20, 719.00, "“Through this *pact* of yaad-faramosh, not only does one get help toward *constant presence*; one also has to admit one's own mistake, and your work becomes *lillah, fillah*.”"),
    (721.20, 738.50, "How far it has taken us! *Lillah*: for the sake of Allah. *Fillah*: in Allah, it turns our attention into Allah; “fi” means “in”."),
    (738.90, 754.10, "“Through this pact of yaad-faramosh, not only does one get help toward constant presence; one also has to admit one's own mistake, and your work becomes *lillah, fillah*.”"),
    (755.30, 772.20, "So this yaad-faramosh means: we will not forget Allah. What was this pact? That we *admit* our own mistake, and then our bond with Allah is *established*, for the sake of Allah."),
    (773.80, 800.40, "“Besides this, for *daf'-e-khatrat*, driving away stray thoughts, more *tried and tested* methods are given below.”"),
    (800.40, 806.60, "Mujarrab: proven, methods that bring *benefit*."),
    (806.80, 816.30, "Now he tells the methods: to drive away *khatrat*, what should one do, what should one read? I am reading them to you."),
    (817.10, 834.70, "“*Number 1*: when khatrat come in great numbers, *change your state*.” If you are sitting, stand up; if standing, walk; or sit down."),
    (835.30, 849.90, "“The root principle here is *movement*.” As soon as we move, the khatrat are driven away."),
    (850.10, 866.90, "We sit, and khatrat keep coming; we sit and sit, and they do not leave. The moment we *stand up* and change our state, the khatrat *vanish*."),
    (867.70, 886.10, "“*Number 2*: when a khatra comes into your heart, into your thoughts, *run* from it.” As soon as it comes, drive yourself away from it: leave it behind, and run."),
    (887.10, 903.90, "“When a khatra comes into your heart, run from it. In a little while the thought will be left behind; you will get out and be *freed* from it.”"),
    (904.90, 916.70, "We who were *captives* of the thought get out of that captivity. You will get out, be released, be free of it."),
    (917.50, 924.70, "“Or imagine that this thought is *running away* from you, in the world of imagination: this too removes it.”"),
    (925.20, 945.70, "That is another way: think, “This thought is running from me.” *Aalam-e-takhayyul* means the world of imagination, of thought. By this too the khatra is *removed*."),
    (976.20, 992.90, "Come into the *refuge* of Allah. The way to come into Allah's refuge is this: “Allah Mian, these khatrat keep coming, so many. Please *help* me and take my khatrat away. I want to come into Your *refuge*.”"),
    (993.30, 1012.70, "When a khatra comes, say: “If these trouble us, where else will we find refuge? Save us from these khatrat; take us into *Your refuge*.” That is how it is."),
    (1014.20, 1028.90, "“*Number 4*: saying “*hoon, hoon*”, breathe out *forcefully*, outward.” Hoon… hoon… hoon… hoon. That is what to do."),
    (1030.30, 1039.30, "To drive away khatrat, what should you do? Saying “hoon, hoon”, breathe out *forcefully*, outward."),
    (1040.10, 1055.80, "You see? Even great people are *troubled* by khatrat. What do you think? The *Awliya Allah* are troubled too; then they come into Allah's refuge, and they adopt all these methods."),
    (1056.80, 1072.90, "Now, “*Number 5*: when a khatra comes, the great ones say: Very well, you have come. You came in *changing forms*: so what? I certainly *recognise* you. The *real existence* is You.”"),
    (1073.70, 1094.10, "*Asl-e-wujood*, the real existence. Whose is the real existence? *Allah's*. Existence is His, and it is He who grants the honour of existence."),
    (1094.40, 1096.50, "This is the matter of very *great* people."),
    (1119.20, 1136.00, "But number 5: when a khatra comes, the great ones say: “Very well, you have come. You came in changing forms: so what? I certainly recognise you.”"),
    (1142.00, 1143.70, "“The real existence is You.”"),
    (1144.70, 1164.10, "In everything, whose is the existence? *Allah's*. Nothing has an existence of its own; existence is *His* alone."),
    (1166.00, 1177.20, "When a khatra comes, the great ones say: “Very well, you have come. You came in changing forms: so what? I certainly recognise you. The real existence is You.”"),
    (1178.80, 1192.40, "“*Bad kaun hai aur nek hai kaun?*”: who is bad, and who is good? This is Hazrat's *rubai*. “*Tu dar har shaan ba-kamaali*”: You are perfect in every state."),
    (1193.80, 1204.70, "“Tu dar har shaan ba-kamaali. *Jis rang mein aao*, kuch nahin hai parwa; is naaz-o-ada se tumko *pehchanta hoon*”: in whatever colour You come, it does not matter; I recognise You by this grace."),
    (1206.10, 1225.10, "That is: whose is the real existence? *Allah's* alone. Now this is such a thing that one has to rise very high for it. It is no *ordinary* thing; it belongs to a very great *station*."),
    (1228.30, 1240.70, "The very great ones: if something comes before them like this to *test* them, and they are deceived, then they are lost. Finished."),
    (1243.90, 1260.90, "So what do they do? “You came in changing forms: so what? I certainly recognise you.” They say it and fall silent: “Yes, I have understood; I have passed my *test*.”"),
    (1262.20, 1287.70, "Who is bad and who is good? You are perfect in every state; in everything there is *perfection*. In whatever colour You come, it does not matter; I recognise You by this grace. *Hasrat*."),
    (1289.80, 1308.80, "And there is a couplet of Hasrat too: “If You meet me in the masjid, I *accept* You; if You meet me in the mandir, I *recognise* You.” This is a very *lofty* matter; an ordinary man cannot understand it."),
    (1316.40, 1335.30, "In the masjid he has accepted: what is he doing? Praying namaz. “In the mandir I *know* You: You are here”; but he does not accept there. He knows, but does not accept. Where does he accept? In the *masjid*. Who is everywhere? *Allah*."),
    (1335.30, 1349.60, "You have read so many books, so you can understand it a little. Not every person can *understand* this."),
    (1352.00, 1365.90, "He tests them in every way; and those who come under the influence of *Shaytan* get confused."),
    (1381.60, 1393.30, "Who is bad and who is good? You are *perfect* in every form. In whatever form You come, it does not matter: I *recognise* You by this grace."),
    (1395.60, 1414.30, "“*Number 6*: by reciting this *verse* too, khatrat lessen.” What is it? “If He wills, He will take you away and bring a *new creation*; and that is not difficult for Allah.”"),
]
